#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342070 :question/problem "Find $t$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{p}=⟨0,3,6⟩$, $\\mathbf{q}=⟨t,t,2⟩$, and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨-1,2,7⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{r}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ t & t & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}t & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}t & 2 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{vmatrix}+6\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}t & t \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0-3(t\\cdot 7-(-1)\\cdot 2)+6(t\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot t) \\\\  & =-21t-6+12t+6t \\\\  & =-3t-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{r}) & =0 \\\\ -3t-6 & =0 \\\\ -3t & =6 \\\\ t & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $t=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342071 :question/problem "Find $t$ given that the vectors $\\mathbf{p}=⟨t,1,t⟩$, $\\mathbf{q}=⟨2,-1,-1⟩$, and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨3,0,1⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{r}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}t & 1 & t \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =t\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+t\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =t((-1)\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-1))-(2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot (-1))+t(2\\cdot 0-3\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =-t-5+3t \\\\  & =2t-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{r}) & =0 \\\\ 2t-5 & =0 \\\\ 2t & =5 \\\\ t & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $t=\\frac{5}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342075 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$, given the points $A(1,1,0)$, $B(-2,1,0)$, $C(3,2,0)$, and $D(1,2,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i} \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+1\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨2,1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 0)-0+0 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-9|=9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342076 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$, given the points $A(1,-1,2)$, $B(-2,2,3)$, $C(1,1,1)$, and $D(-1,3,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-2\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2\\mathbf{i}+4\\mathbf{j}-4\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-2,4,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -4\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3(2\\cdot (-4)-4\\cdot (-1))-3(0\\cdot (-4)-(-2)\\cdot (-1))+(0\\cdot 4-(-2)\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =12+6+4 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|22|=22.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342077 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$, given the points $A(0,1,2)$, $B(-1,0,1)$, $C(1,1,0)$, and $D(3,-1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})-(\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,0,-2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(0\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot (-2))+(1\\cdot 1-3\\cdot (-2))-(1\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =4+7+2 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|13|=13.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342078 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$, given the points $A(2,-1,0)$, $B(-1,2,-1)$, $C(1,1,2)$, and $D(3,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-3(2\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 2)-3((-1)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 2)-((-1)\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-6+9+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|\\,5\\,|=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342081 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$, given the points $A(-2,1,1)$, $B(2,1,0)$, $C(1,-1,2)$, and $D(3,-2,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})-(-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =4\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨4,0,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =\\mathbf{d}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k})-(-2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =5\\mathbf{i}-3\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨5,-3,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}-0\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{vmatrix}+(-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4((-2)\\cdot (-3)-1\\cdot (-3))-0-(3\\cdot (-3)-5\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =36-1 \\\\  & =35.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|35|=35.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342146 :question/problem "Calculate  $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+8)\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+8)\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+8)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+8)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+8)\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+8)}{2}-\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+8)}{2}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+8)\\text{d}x & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+8)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+8)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+8)+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+8)}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342147 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int (6x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int (6x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=6x+1\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=6 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (6x+1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =(6x+1)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot 6\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (6x+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =(6x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-6\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(6x+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342148 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 10x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 10x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=10x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=10 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 10x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =10x\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))\\cdot 10\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 10x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =10x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-10\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344992 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,2)}\\operatorname{ln}(4-x-y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-x-y).\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when $4-x-y$  is negative or zero. However, at the point $(1,2)$,  the expression  $4-x-y$ is positive, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(1,2)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,2)}\\operatorname{ln}(4-x-y) &=\\operatorname{ln}(4-1-2)=\\operatorname{ln}(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344993 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-2,4)}\\sqrt[3]{xy}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{xy}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So,  we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-2,4)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-2,4)}\\sqrt[3]{xy} &=\\sqrt{(-2)\\cdot 4}=\\sqrt[3]{-8}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344994 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{x{z}^{2}-y{z}^{2}}{yz-xz}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{x{z}^{2}-y{z}^{2}}{yz-xz}=\\frac{1\\cdot {2}^{2}-1\\cdot {2}^{2}}{1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{x{z}^{2}-y{z}^{2}}{yz-xz} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1,2)}\\frac{(x-y){z}^{2}}{(y-x)z} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1,2)}-\\frac{(x-y){z}^{2}}{(x-y)z} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1,2)}(-z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1,2)}(-z) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345000 :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}=\\frac{(-1)^{2}+2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}{{1}^{2}-(-1)^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+y)^{2}}{(y-x)(y+x)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{(x+y)(x+y)}{(y-x)(x+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+y}{y-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{x+y}{y-x} & =\\frac{(-1)+1}{1-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345001 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{1+2}}{3-1-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2}}{x-y-2} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y+2})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-(\\sqrt{y+2})^{2}}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,1)}\\frac{x-y-2}{(x-y-2)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y+2}} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{1+2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345002 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-y}{x-{y}^{2}}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-y}{x-{y}^{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{1}-1}{1-{1}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-y}{x-{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-y)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x})^{2}-{y}^{2}}{(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{x-{y}^{2}}{(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+y} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345003 :question/problem "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y}=\\frac{{1}^{2}-1}{\\sqrt{1}-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{{y}^{2}-x}{\\sqrt{x}-y} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{({y}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{(\\sqrt{x}-y)(\\sqrt{x}+y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{({y}^{2}-x)(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}\\frac{-(x-{y}^{2})(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{x-{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}-(\\sqrt{x}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1)}-(\\sqrt{x}+y) & =-(\\sqrt{1}+1) \\\\  & =-(1+1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345004 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}=\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}-0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$” is true. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x(2x-1)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{2x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot 0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $2$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$” is also true. Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y={x}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit exists and is equal to $2$” is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345005 :question/problem "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot 0\\cdot {0}^{3}}{{0}^{6}+8\\cdot {0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{6}+8{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}({x}^{4}+8)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+8} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y={x}^{3}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{{x}^{6}+8{x}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{9{x}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345141 :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=16$ and ${b}^{2}=9.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=16,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be  $a=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=9,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{9}=3.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=4,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=3.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 4=8,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 3=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345157 :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=16$ and ${b}^{2}=25.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=16,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=25,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=5,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=4.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 5=10,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 4=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345158 :question/problem "![](images/bc/bcac8eb60e0603ee38bd200b15072685a2bcebb834d490fb4e5a0c318f401096.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=1$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{1}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345178 :question/problem "![](images/20/202dec2a2e75aa6720181eb925f98999031453f8f1f8822dd86d7a2baab21396.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=3$ and the vertical radius has length $b=5.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345213 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $f(x,y,z)=2x-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$ and the curve $C$ is parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}t-1,\\hspace{0.222em}t+1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,3]?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}t-1,\\hspace{0.222em}t+1⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x={t}^{2},$ $y=t-1,$ and $z=t+1.$ \n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=2x-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =2({t}^{2})-(t-1)^{2}-(t+1)^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-({t}^{2}-2t+1)-({t}^{2}+2t+1) \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}-{t}^{2}+2t-1-{t}^{2}-2t-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2)\\cdot \\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2)\\cdot \\sqrt{2(2{t}^{2}+1)}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2)\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345216 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}y{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $y=5x+2$ for $\\,0\\le x\\le 1?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=5t+2.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,5t+2⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le 1\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t+2)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=y{e}^{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(5t+2){e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\cdot \\sqrt{26}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5t+2){e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345217 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4},$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 4\\sqrt{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le 4\\sqrt{6}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}\\right)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{t}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}\\right)}{t}=\\frac{t}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}\\frac{t}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}t\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}$, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{t}{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\sqrt{6}}t\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4}}\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{25}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}^{3/2}}{3}{|}_{1}^{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({25}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(125-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{124}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345218 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ then along the curve we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$  \n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+4{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={y}^{2}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & ={(2\\operatorname{sin}t)}^{2}=4{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}4{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\left(\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}2t}{2}\\right)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}2t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{[t-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}2t}{2}]}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4[(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\frac{1}{2})-0] \\\\  & =\\pi -2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345219 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-x}$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\hspace{0.222em}t-4⟩$ for $t\\in [0,4].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\hspace{0.222em}t-4⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=t$ and $y=t-4.$     \n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-4)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-x}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{1}{t-(4-t)}=-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\cdot \\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}t\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\cdot (4-0) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345220 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}({x}^{3}-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=4{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $0\\le t\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}(t)=4{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ then along the curve we have $x=4{t}^{2}$ and $y=3{t}^{2}.$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2})\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =8t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+6t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(8t)^{2}+(6t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64{t}^{2}+36{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =10|t| \\\\  & =10t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ since $t>0$ along the path of $C.$ \n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-2{y}^{3}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & ={(4{t}^{2})}^{3}-2{(3{t}^{2})}^{3} \\\\  & ={4}^{3}{t}^{6}-2\\cdot {3}^{3}{t}^{6} \\\\  & =64{t}^{6}-54{t}^{6} \\\\  & =10{t}^{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{3}-{y}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}10{t}^{6}\\cdot 10t\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =100{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{7}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{{t}^{8}}{8}\\hspace{0.167em}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =100[\\frac{{1}^{8}}{8}+\\frac{{0}^{8}}{8}] \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =12.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345223 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$  along the curve $C$ parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)={t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $0<t<\\sqrt{\\pi /2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}(t)={t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ then along the curve we have $x={t}^{2}$ and $y=-{t}^{2}.$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(-2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}|t| \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ because $t>0$ along the path of $C.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}{t}^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\hspace{0.167em}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{2}$, $\\text{d}u=2t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\,t\\,\\text{d}t & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345224 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{1}{x},$ $\\,1\\le x\\le \\sqrt[4]{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $y=y(x).$ Therefore, by setting $x=t,$ we have $y=y(t)=\\frac{1}{t}.$ This gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{1}{t}⟩,\\hspace{2em}1\\le t\\le \\sqrt[4]{2}\n$$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{\\left(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{4{\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)}^{2}}{{t}^{3}}=\\frac{4}{{t}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}3{x}^{5}{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[4]{2}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[4]{2}}\\frac{4}{{t}^{5}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[4]{2}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{5}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}$, $\\text{d}u=-\\frac{4}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt[4]{2}}\\frac{1}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\int }_{2}^{3/2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{2}^{3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{2}^{3}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{4}-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(8\\sqrt{2}-3\\sqrt{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345225 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{5},$ along the curve parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}-3\\operatorname{sin}t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{sin}t,\\hspace{0.222em}-3\\operatorname{sin}t⟩,$ then along the curve we have $x=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$   \n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3\\operatorname{sin}t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}+(-3\\operatorname{cos}t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}+9{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t} \\\\  & =5|\\operatorname{cos}t| \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\ge 0$ when $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{5}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{16{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+9{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{25{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}{5} \\\\  & =5{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}5{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =25{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =25{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =25\\cdot {\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =25(\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345228 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{y+z}{x}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\n$$\nfor $1<t<\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},\n$$ then along the curve we have $x=t,$ $y=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2},$ and $z=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}.$\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{y+z}{x}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}}{t}=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y+z}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+2{t}^{2}$, $\\text{d}u=4t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{3}^{5}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}({5}^{3/2}-{3}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}-3\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345230 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=xy$ along the line segment from $(1,2)$ to $(4,7).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,2)$ to $(4,7).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+7\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})+t[4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+7\\mathbf{j}-(\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})] \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})+t(3\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =(1+3t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2+5t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+3t$ and $y=2+5t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+3t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+5t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =3\\mathbf{i}+5\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(1+3t)(2+5t) \\\\  & =15{t}^{2}+11t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(15{t}^{2}+11t+2)\\cdot \\sqrt{34}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(15{t}^{2}+11t+2)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}{[5{t}^{3}+\\frac{11}{2}{t}^{2}+2t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}(5+\\frac{11}{2}+2)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{25\\sqrt{34}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345231 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s\n$$ along the line segment from $(1,2,0)$ to $(2,1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from$(1,2,0)$ to $(2,1,3).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})+t[(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})-(\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})] \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})+t(\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =(1+t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(2-t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+3t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+t,$ $y=2-t,$ and $z=3t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(1+t)(2-t)^{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4)\\cdot \\sqrt{11}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}-{t}^{3}+4t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}(\\frac{1}{4}-1+4)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\sqrt{11}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345232 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}(x+z))\\,\\text{d}s\n$$ along the line segment from $(-1,2,1)$ to $(1,2,5).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,2,1)$ to $(1,2,5).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=-\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})+t[(\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})-(-\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})] \\\\  & =(-\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})+t(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =(-1+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(1+4t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-1+2t,$ $y=2,$ and $z=1+4t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1+2t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+4t)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+0+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}(x+z))$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}((-1+2t)+(1+4t))) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi t}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}(x+z))\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi t}{2}\\right)\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi t}{2}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\frac{3\\pi t}{2}$, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi t}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }(-1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345236 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi (y-x)}{8}\\right)\\,\\text{d}s\n$$ along the line segment from $(1,1)$ to $(2,4).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,1)$ to $(2,4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})+t[2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})] \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j})+t(\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =(1+t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(1+3t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+t$ and $y=1+3t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+3t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =1\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi (y-x)}{8}\\right)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & ={\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi ((1+3t)-(1+t))}{8}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi (y-x)}{8}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}\\right)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\operatorname{sec}}^{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\frac{\\pi t}{4},$ $\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345241 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(4x+2)\\,{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}s\n$$ along the line segment from $(0,1)$ to $(2,3).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,1)$ to $(2,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $\\mathbf{a}=\\mathbf{j}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}(t) & =\\mathbf{a}+t\\hspace{0.167em}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{j}+t[2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{j}] \\\\  & =\\mathbf{j}+t(2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(1+2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t$ and $y=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=(4x+2)\\,{e}^{xy}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =(4(2t)+2){e}^{2t(1+2t)} \\\\  & =(8t+2)\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{2t+4{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{xy}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+2)\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{2t+4{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+2)\\hspace{0.167em}{e}^{2t+4{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2t+4{t}^{2},$ $\\text{d}u=(2+8t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+2)\\,{e}^{2t+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{u}\\,{|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}({e}^{6}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}({e}^{6}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345259 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}4,\\hspace{0.222em}{t}^{2}⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+0+{(2t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\hspace{0.167em}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $t\\ge 0$ for $t\\in [0,2].$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2\\sqrt{2}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345260 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨t,\\hspace{0.222em}{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}2t,\\hspace{0.222em}2{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(2t)}^{2}+{(2{t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}+4{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(1+2{t}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =1+2{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(1+2{t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(t+\\frac{2{t}^{3}}{3})|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =3+18-0 \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
